# Bengaluru BMTC Route & Schedule Analysis

An interactive data analytics dashboard for exploring Bengaluru Metropolitan Transport Corporation (BMTC) scheduled bus services, stop activity, route activity and stop clusters.

Live Dashboard

[Open the Streamlit Dashboard](https://public-transport-delay-analysis-nj5z6niqsaq4msdftf8ytg.streamlit.app/)

 Project Overview

This project analyses BMTC public transport schedule data using Python, data analysis and machine learning.

The main objective is to understand:
- Which stops have more scheduled service activity?
- Which routes have more scheduled trips?
- How are bus stops distributed geographically?
- Can stops be grouped based on their service activity and location?

Important: This project uses static GTFS schedule data. It does not measure actual bus delays, live arrivals, passenger counts or real-time traffic.

Dataset

The project uses GTFS (General Transit Feed Specification) data.

Main files used:
- `stops.txt` – stop names and geographical coordinates
- `routes.txt` – route information
- `trips.txt` – scheduled trips
- `stop_times.txt` – scheduled arrival and departure times
- `calendar.txt` – service calendar

Dataset source: [BMTC GTFS dataset](https://github.com/anikets95/bmtc-data)

The available calendar snapshot covers **7 November 2024 to 13 November 2024**.

 Technologies Used

- Python
- Pandas
- Plotly
- Streamlit
- Scikit-learn
- GTFS data

 Data Processing and Analysis

The project includes:
1. Loading and exploring GTFS files.
2. Cleaning missing and invalid records.
3. Combining stop, route, trip and stop-time information.
4. Analysing scheduled activity at stops and routes.
5. Exploring geographical distribution using latitude and longitude.
6. Creating stop-level features for clustering.
7. Applying K-Means clustering with K=2.
8. Building an interactive Streamlit dashboard.

 Key Dashboard Findings

The dashboard currently displays:

| Metric | Value |
|---|---:|
| Total stops | 8,305 |
| Scheduled stop visits | 859,957 |
| Average unique routes per stop | 12.6 |
| Top routes in export | 10 |

The scheduled stop visits metric represents the sum of scheduled trip counts across stops, not the number of unique trips.

Top Routes in the Export

The leading routes include:
- 401-A
- 300-MV
- MF-401
- K-2
- 96-G
- V-500D
- G-7
- KIA-9
- V-500CA
- 300-E

 K-Means Stop Clustering

K-Means was applied using stop-level service activity and geographical features.

The selected K=2 model produced:

| Cluster | Number of stops | Average scheduled trips per stop | Average unique routes per stop |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| 0 | 7,398 | 50.80 | 6.57 |
| 1 | 907 | 533.74 | 62.10 |

Interpretation:
- Cluster 0 contains stops with relatively lower scheduled service activity.
- Cluster 1 contains stops with much higher scheduled service activity and route connectivity.

These are analytical interpretations of the clusters, not official BMTC stop categories.

The K=2 model achieved a silhouette score of approximately 0.470. This indicates moderate separation between the clusters, not prediction accuracy.

 Dashboard Features

- Interactive stop-cluster filter
- Key project metrics
- Top 15 stops by scheduled service activity
- Top routes by scheduled trips
- K-Means cluster summary
- Stop distribution map
- Searchable stop details
- CSV download option

 Run the Project Locally

1. Clone the repository

bash
git clone https://github.com/Ashokyadhav/public-transport-delay-analysis.git
cd public-transport-delay-analysis

 2. Install dependencies

bash
pip install -r requirements.txt

3. Run the Streamlit application

bash
streamlit run bmtc_app.py

 Project Files
text
public-transport-delay-analysis/
│
├── bmtc_app.py
├── clustered_stops.csv
├── top_routes.csv
├── requirements.txt
└── bengaluru_transport_delay_analysis.ipynb

 Limitations

- The dataset is a static schedule snapshot covering 7–13 November 2024.
- It does not contain actual bus arrival observations or live vehicle positions.
- Passenger demand and passenger counts are not available.
- Clustering results depend on the selected features and model settings.
- The geographical analysis uses stop coordinates; it does not represent actual road travel distances.

 Future Improvements

- Integrate reliable real-time bus position and arrival data if available.
- Add more historical schedule snapshots for comparison.
- Include passenger demand data if a suitable dataset becomes available.
- Improve geographical and route-level visualisations.
- Explore additional clustering approaches.

Conclusion

This project demonstrates how public transport schedule data can be cleaned, analysed and visualised using Python. It also demonstrates the use of K-Means clustering to identify groups of stops with different levels of scheduled service activity.

The final result is an interactive dashboard that makes BMTC schedule information easier to explore and understand.

